<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taxila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taxila was an ancient city in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, renowned as a centre of trade and learning. It lay on routes connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> name it, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, as one of the urban centres that contributed to craft production, coinage and long-distance commerce.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taxila.jpg" alt="Taxila" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sasha Isachenko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dharmarajika_stupa,Taxila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
